'''Acabats i Tints de Balboa, S.A.''' (ATB) is a Balboan {{w|textile manufacturing|textile wet processing}} and finishing company based in [[Sant Bart]]. The company provides {{w|dyeing}}, {{w|textile printing|printing}}, bleaching, washing, and chemical and mechanical finishing services for textile manufacturers across [[Balboa]] and the [[BTPMEU]] region. It is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] under the ticker ATB and is a constituent of the [[SCX 100]] index.

ATB operates as a business-to-business service provider, processing fabrics on a contract basis for textile manufacturers that do not maintain their own wet processing facilities. The company's clients include domestic manufacturers such as [[Horaci Eduard Molí]] and smaller textile producers, as well as manufacturers in [[Tapuya]] and [[Potocsí]] seeking finishing services within the BTPMEU tariff area.

== History ==

ATB was founded in 1984 by Ramon Serra i Font, a textile engineer who had previously managed the dyeing and finishing division of a canal corridor garment manufacturer. Serra identified that many smaller textile producers in the Sant Bart area lacked the capital to invest in their own wet processing facilities, and that a dedicated service provider could achieve economies of scale by concentrating dyeing, printing, and finishing capacity in a single operation.

The company built its first processing facility in [[Sant Bart]] in 1985, with initial capacity focused on batch {{w|dyeing}} and basic chemical finishing. The facility's location in the Sant Bart textile cluster provided proximity to the company's primary client base while offering access to the [[Badia Curta]] chemical complex for dye and chemical supply.

In the 1990s, ATB expanded into {{w|textile printing|textile printing}}, adding rotary screen printing lines to serve the growing demand for printed fabrics from domestic apparel manufacturers. The establishment of the [[BTPMEU]] in 1998 opened additional contract processing opportunities from textile manufacturers in [[Tapuya]] and [[Potocsí]].

The company was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1997. The [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] acquired a 4 per cent stake in 2008.

In the 2010s, ATB invested in {{w|textile manufacturing|low-water dyeing}} technology and effluent treatment systems, responding to tightening environmental regulations and pressure from BTPMEU-based brands requiring sustainable processing standards.

== Operations ==

ATB operates a single processing facility in [[Sant Bart]], comprising:

* Batch and continuous dyeing lines for woven and knitted fabrics
* Rotary screen printing lines
* Bleaching and washing ranges
* Chemical finishing lines (softening, water-repellent, flame-retardant treatments)
* Mechanical finishing lines (calendering, raising, shrinking)

The company sources dyes and processing chemicals from the [[Badia Curta]] chemical complex, including from [[Companyia Química Occidental]] and [[Grup Rojo]]. Approximately 70 per cent of revenue is derived from domestic clients, with the remaining 30 per cent from BTPMEU-based manufacturers.

